 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# DSA in Python\n",
    "\n",
    "Please excuse any unprofessional writing in this repo \u2014 I didn't build this as a course for others, but as a personal tool to revise Data Structures and Algorithms basics.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Fair warning:** this isn't meant for a complete novice with no programming background. I'm keeping definitions short, informative, and concise rather than over-explaining.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Once you've properly internalized the basic concepts, DSA is honestly no harder than any other subject you've studied before.\n",
    "\n",
    "The sole purpose of this repo is interview prep. Treat it as a **starting point**, then move on to grinding the well-known LeetCode question lists.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Course Structure\n",
    "\n",
    "- Data structures: arrays, linked lists, stacks, queues, trees, heaps, and graphs.\n",
    "- Famous algorithms: Dijkstra's algorithm, Prim's algorithm, and others.\n",
    "- A few tips and tricks as a bonus for some lessons.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Some things you should know\n",
    "\n",
    "- DSA is fundamentally a problem-solving toolkit that helps reduce the time or space complexity of a solution.\n",
    "- What interviewers actually want to see is whether you can recognize which data structure/pattern converts a worse time complexity into a better one.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Exhaustive List of DSA Interview Patterns\n",
    "\n",
    "Most coding interview questions boil down to a handful of recurring patterns. Recognizing *which* pattern applies is 80% of the battle \u2014 the rest is just writing the template correctly.\n",
    "\n",
    "Broadly split into **linear structure patterns**, **nonlinear structure patterns**, and a few **general-purpose patterns** that show up everywhere.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Linear Data Structure Patterns\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Two Pointers**\n",
    "   - Reduces time complexity to linear time O(n).\n",
    "   - *Same direction:* fast & slow pointers \u2014 cycle detection, finding the middle of a linked list.\n",
    "   - *Opposite directions:* pair-sum problems on a sorted array, container-with-most-water style problems.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Sliding Window**\n",
    "   - A refinement of two pointers that maintains a dynamic \"window\" over a range of elements.\n",
    "   - Expands/contracts the window to satisfy a condition (e.g. longest substring without repeating characters).\n",
    "   - Often paired with a hashmap/set to track window contents.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "3. **Binary Search**\n",
    "   - Finds a target in logarithmic time O(log n).\n",
    "   - Not just for sorted arrays \u2014 extends to any search space with a monotonic (yes/no) condition.\n",
    "   - Example: finding the minimum in a rotated sorted array, binary search on the answer.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "4. **Fast & Slow Pointers**\n",
    "   - Special case of two pointers, worth calling out on its own.\n",
    "   - Detects cycles (Floyd's cycle detection), finds the middle node, detects palindromic linked lists.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "5. **Merge Intervals**\n",
    "   - Sort intervals by start time, then merge overlapping ones in a single pass.\n",
    "   - Shows up in scheduling problems, calendar conflicts, interval insertion.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "6. **Cyclic Sort**\n",
    "   - Used when dealing with an array containing numbers in a given range (e.g. 1 to n).\n",
    "   - Places each number at its correct index in-place to find missing/duplicate numbers in O(n) time, O(1) space.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "7. **In-place Reversal of a Linked List**\n",
    "   - Reverses nodes without extra space by manipulating pointers directly.\n",
    "   - Common variant: reverse a sub-list between positions m and n, reverse in groups of k.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "8. **Prefix Sum**\n",
    "   - Precompute cumulative sums so any range-sum query becomes O(1).\n",
    "   - Extends to prefix XOR, prefix product, and 2D prefix sums for matrices.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "9. **Monotonic Stack**\n",
    "   - Maintains a stack that is always increasing or decreasing.\n",
    "   - Used for \"next greater/smaller element\" problems, histogram/largest rectangle problems.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "### Nonlinear Data Structure Patterns\n",
    "\n",
    "10. **Breadth-First Search (BFS)**\n",
    "    - Explores nodes level by level using a queue.\n",
    "    - Ideal for shortest path in unweighted graphs and level-order tree traversal.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "11. **Depth-First Search (DFS)**\n",
    "    - Dives deep into one path before backtracking; usually recursive (or stack-based).\n",
    "    - Memory-efficient for exploring all paths. Example: counting islands in a grid, path sum problems.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "12. **Backtracking**\n",
    "    - An extension of DFS that explores *all* possible solutions and prunes invalid ones.\n",
    "    - Builds a solution incrementally, undoing (\"backtracking\") a choice when it doesn't work out.\n",
    "    - Example: letter combinations of a phone number, N-Queens, subsets/permutations.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "13. **Topological Sort**\n",
    "    - Orders nodes in a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) such that every edge points forward.\n",
    "    - Used for task scheduling with dependencies, course prerequisite problems.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "14. **Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union)**\n",
    "    - Efficiently tracks and merges connected components.\n",
    "    - Used for detecting cycles in undirected graphs, Kruskal's MST algorithm, \"number of provinces\" style problems.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "15. **Trie (Prefix Tree)**\n",
    "    - Tree structure optimized for prefix-based string operations.\n",
    "    - Used for autocomplete, word search, longest common prefix problems.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "### Heaps / Priority Queue\n",
    "\n",
    "16. **Heaps (Top-K pattern)**\n",
    "    - Used for \"top K\", \"K smallest/largest\", \"K closest\" style problems.\n",
    "    - **Min Heap:** smallest value at the root \u2014 used to efficiently track the K *largest* elements seen so far.\n",
    "    - **Max Heap:** largest value at the root \u2014 used to efficiently track the K *smallest* elements seen so far.\n",
    "    - Two-heap variant (min-heap + max-heap) is used for streaming median problems.\n"
    "\n",
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Dynamic Programming & Greedy\n",
    "\n",
    "17. **Dynamic Programming (DP)**\n",
    "    - Optimizes solutions by breaking a problem into overlapping subproblems.\n",
    "    - *Top-down:* recursion + memoization to cache repeated work.\n",
    "    - *Bottom-up:* iteratively fills a table starting from the base cases.\n",
    "    - Common sub-patterns: 0/1 knapsack, unbounded knapsack, longest common subsequence, longest increasing subsequence.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "18. **Greedy Algorithms**\n",
    "    - Makes the locally optimal choice at each step, hoping it leads to a globally optimal solution.\n",
    "    - Works only when the problem has \"optimal substructure\" + \"greedy choice property\" \u2014 e.g. activity selection, Huffman coding, jump game.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "### General-Purpose Patterns\n",
    "\n",
    "19. **Matrix Traversal**\n",
    "    - Grid-based BFS/DFS, spiral traversal, rotating a matrix in place.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "20. **Divide and Conquer**\n",
    "    - Splits a problem into independent subproblems, solves them recursively, then combines results.\n",
    "    - Example: merge sort, quick sort, finding max subarray.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "21. **Bit Manipulation**\n",
    "    - Uses bitwise operators (AND, OR, XOR, shifts) to solve problems in constant extra space.\n",
    "    - Example: single number problems (XOR trick), counting set bits, subsets via bitmasking.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "22. **Graph Coloring / Bipartite Check**\n",
    "    - Uses BFS/DFS with a 2-coloring scheme to check if a graph is bipartite.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "Focus on building pre-built code templates for each of these patterns so that recognizing the pattern in an interview immediately gives you a skeleton to work from.\n"
   ]
  }
